DO'S & DON'TS — QUICK WALKTHROUGH

AREEB: Alright, before everyone disappears into code, we have one very important slide for you.

And don’t worry—

we’re not going to read every bullet word-for-word.

If we wanted to do that, we could have just emailed you the slide.

DHRITI: But there are a few things here that can genuinely save you a lot of pain over the next 24 hours.

So think of this as—

things we wish someone had very clearly reminded us of when we were participants.

---

1. PLAN BEFORE YOU BUILD

AREEB: First one—

please read the problem statement properly before jumping straight into coding.

I know the instinct is:

“We understood 60% of it… let’s start building.”

Maybe don’t.

DHRITI: Take a little time to research, discuss your approach, and divide responsibilities clearly.

Frontend, backend, model, presentation—

know who owns what.

AREEB: Basically—

divide and conquer.

Not—

divide and discover at 2 AM that nobody did the integration.

---

2. BUILD THE CORE FIRST

DHRITI: Next—

please don’t spend three hours making your UI look like the next Apple launch...

while the actual model still doesn’t work.

AREEB: Beautiful gradients are great.

A functioning solution is greater.

Get the core working first.

Then make it pretty.

DHRITI: And don’t overcommit.

You don’t need seventeen features.

A smaller solution that actually works will always beat an ambitious one that exists mostly in your imagination.

---

3. SAVE. BACK UP. DOCUMENT.

AREEB: This one is simple, but very important.

Keep saving your work.

Back it up.

And please—

document what you’re doing.

DHRITI: Because there is no character development quite like losing three hours of work...

and realising your last save was before lunch.

AREEB: Save early.

Save often.

Back up.

Your future self will thank you.

---

4. TEST WHILE YOU BUILD

DHRITI: Please don’t leave testing for Hour 22.

If you can, test as you build.

AREEB: Because at Hour 22, you should ideally be polishing your pitch...

not discovering that the feature you’ve been confidently talking about all day has never worked end-to-end.

DHRITI: Catch problems early.

Future-you deserves that kindness.

---

5. ASK FOR HELP

AREEB: And please use the mentors.

Don’t sit stuck for two hours because you think asking a question somehow means you know less.

DHRITI: Sometimes a ten-minute conversation can save you from going in a completely wrong direction.

Ask questions.

Take feedback.

Use the expertise in the room.

AREEB: And network as well.

Don’t spend all 24 hours speaking only to the three people sitting at your table.

---

6. TAKE CARE OF YOURSELF

DHRITI: Also—

eat.

Hydrate.

Stretch.

Take short breaks.

AREEB: Coffee is useful.

Coffee is not a personality, a meal plan, and a hydration strategy combined.

[Pause.]

Your solution needs to survive the hackathon.

So do you.

---

7. SUBMISSION & PRESENTATION

DHRITI: And now, possibly the most important operational point—

submit on time.

AREEB: Not—

“It's uploading.”

Not—

“Just one final change.”

Not—

“We thought the deadline was flexible.”

DHRITI: On. Time.

And make the submission stand out.

You’ve spent nearly 24 hours building it—

do justice to it when you present it.

AREEB: One very important instruction for your final pitch:

please use Microsoft PowerPoint only.

No Google Slides.

No last-minute format adventures.

DHRITI: And make sure your required software is installed and ready well in advance.

The middle of a 24-hour hackathon is not the ideal moment to discover—

“Oh... I don’t actually have this installed.”

---

WRAP-UP

AREEB: So if we had to compress this entire slide into one sentence—

DHRITI: Understand before you build.

AREEB: Build before you decorate.

DHRITI: Save before you regret.

AREEB: Ask before you get stuck.

DHRITI: Test before Hour 22.

AREEB: And—

BOTH: Submit on time.

AREEB: Do that...

and you’ve already avoided half the drama of a hackathon.